id: design.shared-state
kind: design
title: Shared state
aliases: shared mutable state, shared state, state sharing
tags: concurrency, architecture
questions:
  - how do you share state between concurrent workers?
invariant: Shared mutable state is a design choice with a cost model; the alternatives are ownership transfer, immutable snapshots and single-owner serialization.
answer_30s: Prefer no shared state: move ownership to one task, pass immutable snapshots, or serialize writes through a single owner (actor or channel) -- and use a lock only for a small, genuinely shared mutation.
answer_2m: |
  Four designs, in the order to consider them: (1) no sharing, each worker has
  its own state (stateless services, partition by key); (2) ownership transfer,
  where the message or job carries the data and only one task holds it (channels,
  actors); (3) immutable snapshot sharing, where readers get a new version
  atomically (Arc plus copy-on-write, watch channel for the latest version);
  (4) a lock around a small mutation, when the state is truly shared and the
  critical section is short. The interview mistake to avoid is jumping to (4) for
  everything: a global lock converts a horizontally scalable service into an
  effectively single-threaded one, and it usually appears as latency that no
  amount of autoscaling fixes. Also remember the horizontal version of the same
  question: in-process sharing does not extend across replicas, so a local cache
  or counter must be reconciled with the shared source of truth.
when_to_use:
  - deciding the concurrency model of a service, worker or controller
avoid_when:
  - global locks on a hot path, or shared mutable state across replicas without a coordination plan
failure_modes:
  - a lock protecting a large critical section, serializing a scalable service
  - per-replica state assumed to be global (counters, caches, rate limits)
  - two writers to the same state with no single owner, so they overwrite each other
related:
  - rust.arc
  - rust.mutex
  - rust.rwlock
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - rust.app-state
  - design.worker-pool
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch16-00-concurrency.html
